\begin_layout Title
Chapter 6: Additional Single-Equation Topics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Control Function Approach, Over-Identification Test
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
6.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Control Function Approach to Endogeneity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For several reasons, including handling endogeneity in nonlinear models,
 it is very useful to have a different approach for dealing with endogenous
 variables.
 Generally, the 
\series bold
control function
\series default
 approach uses extra regressors to break the correlation between endogenous
 explanatory variables and unobservables affecting the response.
 As we will see, the method still relies on the availability of exogenous
 varaibles that do not appear in the structural equation.
 For notational clarity, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 denotes the response variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is the endogenous explanatory variable (a scalar for simplicity), and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of exogenous variables (which includes unity as its first element).
 Consider the model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{1i}=z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\alpha_{1}y_{2i}+u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{1}\times1$
\end_inset

 strict subvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 that also includes a constant.
 The sense in which 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous is given by the 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 orthogonality (zero covariance) conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(z_{i}u_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Of course, this is the same exogeneity condition we use for consistency
 of the 2SLS estimator, and we can consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 by 2SLS under (2) and the rank condition, Assumption 2SLS.2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Just as with 2SLS, the reduced form of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

, i.e., the linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 onto the exogenous variables, plays a crucial role.
 Write the reduced form with an error term as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y_{2i} & = & z_{i}^{\prime}\pi_{2}+v_{i}\\
E(z_{i}v_{i}) & = & 0
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{2}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

.
 From equations (3) and (4), endogeneity of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 arises if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Write the linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

, in error form, as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u_{i}=\rho_{1}v_{i}+e_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{1}=\nicefrac{E(v_{i}u_{i})}{E(v_{i}^{2})}$
\end_inset

 is the population regression coefficient.
 By definition, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(v_{i}e_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}e_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 are both uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Plugging (5) into equation (1) gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{1i}=z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\alpha_{1}y_{2i}+\rho_{1}v_{i}+e_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where we now view 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 as an explanatory variable in the equation.
 As just noted, 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Plus, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is a linear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

, and so 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is also uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Because 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

, equation (6) suggests a simple procedure for consistently estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 (as well as 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{1})$
\end_inset

: run the OLS regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 using a random sample.
 (Remember, OLS consistently estimates the parameters in any equation where
 the error term is uncorrelated with the right-hand-side variables.) The
 only problem with this suggestion is that we do not obverve 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{2i}$
\end_inset

; it is the error of the reduced-form equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

.
 Nevertheless, we can write 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=y_{2i}-z_{i}^{\prime}\pi_{2}$
\end_inset

, and, because we collect data on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, we can consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{2}$
\end_inset

 by OLS.
 Therefore, we can replace 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

, the OLS residuals from the first-stage regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Simple substitution gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{1i}=z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\alpha_{1}y_{2i}+\rho_{1}\hat{v}_{i}+\mbox{\emph{error}}_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where, fore each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{error}}_{i}=e_{i}+\rho_{1}z_{i}^{\prime}(\hat{\pi}_{2}-\pi_{2})$
\end_inset

, which depends on the sampling error in 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\pi}_{2}$
\end_inset

 unless 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{1}=0$
\end_inset

.
 We can now use the consistency result for generated regressors from Section
 6.1.1 to conclude that the OLS estimators from equation (7) will be consistent
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The OLS estimator from equation (7) is an example of a 
\series bold
control function (CF)
\series default
 
\series bold
estimator
\series default
.
 The inclusion of the residuals 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

controls
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for the endogeneity of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 in the original equation (although it does so with sampling error because
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\pi}_{2}\neq\pi_{2}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

How does the CF approach compare with the standard instrumental variables
 (IV) approach, which, for now, means 2SLS? Interestingly, the OLS estimates
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 from equation (7) are 
\emph on
identical 
\emph default
to the 2SLS estimates.
 In other words, the two approaches lead to the sample place.
 Equation (7) contains a generated regressor, and so obtaining the appropriate
 standard errors for the CF estimators requires applying the correction
 derived in Appendix 6A.
 Why, then, have we introduced the control funciton approach when it leads
 to the same place as 2SLS?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There are a few reasons.
 First, equation (7) leads to a straightforward test of the null hypothesisi
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous.
 Second, and perhaps more important, the CF approach can be adapted to certain
 nonlinear models in cases where analogues of 2SLS have undesirable properties.
 Finally, although we just argued that the CF approach is identical to 2SLS
 in a linear model, this equivalence does not carry over to models contianing
 more than one function of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

You have perhaps already figured out where the CF approch relies on having
 at least one element of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 not also in 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

, as also required by 2SLS.
 We can easily see this from equation (7) and the expression 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}=y_{2i}-z_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\pi}_{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}=z_{1i}$
\end_inset

, then the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

 are exact linear functions of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

, in which case equation (7) sufferes from perfect collinearity.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 contains at least one element in 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

, this breaks the perfect collinearity (at least in the sample).
 If we write 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}=(z_{1i},z_{2i})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{22}=0$
\end_inset

, then, asymptotically, the CF approach suffers from perfect collinearity,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{1}$
\end_inset

 are not identified.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
6.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Some Specification Tests
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
6.3.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Testing for Endogeneity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There are various ways to motivate test for whether some explanatory variables
 are endogenous.
 If the null hypothesis is that all explanatory variables are exogenous,
 and we allow one or more to be endogenous under the alternative, then we
 can base a test on the difference between the 2SLS and OLS estimators,
 provided we have sufficient exogenous instruments to identify the parameters
 by 2SLS.
 In the general equation 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}$
\end_inset

 with instruments 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, the 
\series bold
Durbin-Wu-Hausman (DWH)
\series default
 test is based on the difference 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}-\hat{\beta}_{OLS}$
\end_inset

.
 If all elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 are exogenous (and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is also exogenous, a maintained assumption), then 2SLS and OLS should differ
 only due to sampling error.
 Of course, to determine whether this is so, we need to estimate the asymptotic
 variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}-\hat{\beta}_{OLS})$
\end_inset

.
 Generally, the calculation is combersome, but is simplifies considerably
 if we maintain homoskedasticity under the null hypothesis.
 We can verfiy that, under the null hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, and the appropriate homosekdasticity assumption,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{AVar}\left[\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}-\hat{\beta}_{OLS}\right)\right]=\sigma^{2}\left[E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{*\prime})\right]^{-1}-\sigma^{2}\left[E(x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is simply the difference between the asymptotic variances.
 It has been shown that this kind of result holds more generally when one
 estimator, OLS, in this case, is asymptotically efficient under the null.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Once we estimate each component in equation (8) we can construnct a quadratic
 form in 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}-\hat{\beta}_{OLS}$
\end_inset

 that has an asymptotic chi-square distribution.
 The estimators of the moment matrices are the usual ones, with the first-stage
 fitted values 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{i}^{\prime}=z_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Pi}$
\end_inset

 replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*\prime}$
\end_inset

, as usual.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

, there are three possibilities.
 The first is to estimate the rror variances separately for 2SLS and OLS
 and insert these in the first and second occurrences of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

, respectively.
 Or we can just use the 2SLS estimate or just use the OLS estimate in both
 places.
 Using the latter, we obtain one version of the DWH statistic:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\hat{\sigma}_{OLS}^{2}}\left(\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}-\hat{\beta}_{OLS}\right)^{\prime}\left[\left(\hat{X}^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}-\left(X^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}\right]^{-1}\left(\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}-\hat{\beta}_{OLS}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where we must use a generalized inverse, except in the very usual case that
 all elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 are allowed to be endogenous under the alternatie.
 In fact, the rank of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{AVar}\left[\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}-\hat{\beta}_{OLS}\right)\right]$
\end_inset

 is equal to the number of elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 allowed to be endogenous under the alternative.
 The singularity of the matrix in expression (9) makes computing the statistic
 cumbersome.
 Nevertheless, some variant of the statistic is routinely computed by several
 popular econometrics packages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A more serious drawback with any statistic based on equation (8) is that
 it is not robut to heteroskedasticity.
 A robut variance matrix estimator for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{AVar}\left[\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}-\hat{\beta}_{OLS}\right)\right]$
\end_inset

 can be obtained.
 Unfortunately, calculating robust DWH statistics is doen much less frequently
 than computing standard errors and classical test statistics that are robust
 to heteroskedasticity.
 Infrequent use of a fully robust DWH statistic may be partly due to misundersta
ndings of when the principle of comparing estimators applies.
 It is commonly thought that the estimator should be asymptotically efficient
 under the null hypothesis, but this is not necessary; indeed, it is essentially
 irrelevant.
 The principle applies whenever two estimators are consisitent under the
 null hypothesis and one estimator, 2SLS in this case, retains consistency
 under the alternative.
 Asymptotic efficiency only simplifies the asymptotic variance estimation.
 In fact, while the standard form of the DWH test maintains OLS.3 and 2SLS.3
 in order to have the correct asymptotic size, it has no systematic power
 for detecting heteroskedasticity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Regression-based endogeneity tests are very convenient because tey are easily
 computed and almost trivial to make robust to heteroskedasticity.
 There is a regression-based statistic asymptotically equivalent to equation
 (1).
 Suppose initially that we have a single potentailly endogenous explanatory
 variable.
 We maintain assumption (2), that is, each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous.
 We want to test
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H_{0}:\mbox{Cov}(y_{2i},u_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

against the alternative that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Given the reduced-form equation (3), equation (10) is equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(v_{i},u_{i})=E(v_{i}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}=\rho_{1}=0$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the regression coefficient defined in equation (5).
 Even though 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 are not observed, we can use equation (7) to test that 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{1}=0$
\end_inset

.
 In fact, when we estimate equation (7) by OLS, we obtain an OLS coefficient
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\rho}_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Conveniently, when 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{1}=0$
\end_inset

 we do not have to adjust the standard error of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\rho}_{1}$
\end_inset

 for the first-stage estimation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, if the homoskedasticity assumption 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}^{2}|z_{i},y_{2i})=E(u_{i}^{2})$
\end_inset

 holds under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

, we can use the usual 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistic for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\rho}_{1}$
\end_inset

 as an asymptotically valid test.
 Conveniently, we can simply use a heteroskedasticity-robust 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistic if heteroskedasticity is suspected under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We should remember that the OLS standard errors that would be reported from
 equation (7) are not valid unless 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{1}=0$
\end_inset

, because 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a generated regressor.
 In practice, if we reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\rho_{1}=0$
\end_inset

, then, to get the appropriate standard errors and other test statistics,
 we estimate equation (1) by 2SLS.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
6.3.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Testing Overidentifying Restrictions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When we have more instruments than we need to identify an equation, we can
 test whether the additional instruments are valid in the sense that they
 are uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 To explain the various procedures, write the equation in the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{1i}=z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+y_{2i}^{\prime}\alpha_{1}+u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{1}\times1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{1}\times1$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of all exogenous variables is again 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

; partition this as 
\begin_inset Formula $z=(z_{1i},z_{2i})$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{2}\times1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L=L_{1}+L_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Because the model is overidentified, 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{2}>G_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Under the usual identification conditions we could use any 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{1}\times1$
\end_inset

 subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{2i}$
\end_inset

 as instruments for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 in estimating equation (11) (remember the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

 act as their own instruments).
 Following this general principle, Hausman suggested comparing the 2SLS
 estimator using all instruments to 2SLS using a subset that just identifies
 equation (11).
 If all instruments are valid, the estimates should differ only as a result
 of sampling error.
 As with testing for endogeneity, the Hausman test based on a quadratic
 form in the coefficient differences can be cumbsersome to compute.
 Fortunately, a regression-based test is available.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Sargan test maintains homoskedasticity (Assumption 2SLS.3) under the
 null hypothesis.
 It is easily obtained as 
\begin_inset Formula $NR_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 from the OLS regression
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{u}_{i}\ \mbox{on}\ z_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 are the 2SLS residuals using 
\emph on
all
\emph default
 of the instruments 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the usual 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

-squared (assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 contain a constant; otherwise it is the uncentered 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

-squared).
 In other words, simply estimate regression (11) by 2SLS and obtain the
 2SLS residuals, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Then regress these on all exogenous variables (including a constant).
 Under the null that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 and Assumption 2SLS.3 
\begin_inset Formula $NR_{u}^{2}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{a}\\
\sim
\end{array}\chi_{Q_{1}}^{2}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{1}\equiv L_{2}-G_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the number of overidentifying restrictions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The usefulness of the Sargan-Hausman test is that, if we reject the null
 hypothesis, then our logic for choosing the IVs must be reexamined.
 Unfortunately, the test does not tell us which IVs fail the exogeneity
 requirement; it could be one of them or all of them.
 (The symmetric way that all exogenous variables appear in regression in
 (12) makes it clear the test cannot single out faulty instruments.) If we
 fail to reject the null hypothesis, then we can have some confidence in
 the set of instruments used, up to a point.
 Even if we do not reject the null hypothesis, it is possible that more
 than one isntrument is endogenous, and that the 2SLS estimators using a
 full and reduced set of instruments are asymptotically biased in similar
 ways.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is straightforward to obtain a heteroskedasticity-robust test of the
 over-identifying restrictions, but we need to separate the instrumental
 variables into two groups.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{2i}$
\end_inset

 be the 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{2}\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of exogenous variables excluded
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{1i}=z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+y_{2i}^{\prime}\alpha_{1}+u_{i},\ E(z_{i}u_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and write 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{2i}=(g_{2i},h_{2i})$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{1}\times1$
\end_inset

, the same dimension as 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{1}\times1$
\end_inset

, the number of overidentifying restrictions.
 It turns out not to matter how we do this division, provided 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{2i}$
\end_inset

 has 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{1}$
\end_inset

 elements.
 As in equation (12), let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the 2SLS residuals form estimating equation (13), and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{2i}$
\end_inset

 denot the fitted values form the first stage regression, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 (each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 onto 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

).
 Next, regress each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{2i}$
\end_inset

 onto 
\begin_inset Formula $(z_{1i},\hat{y}_{2i})$
\end_inset

 and save the residuals, say 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{r}_{i}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{1}\times1$
\end_inset

 for each observation).
 Then the LM statistic obtained as 
\begin_inset Formula $N-SSR_{0}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the sum of squared residuals from the regression 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}\hat{r}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

, and without assuming homoskedasticity, 
\begin_inset Formula $N-SSR_{0}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{a}\\
\sim
\end{array}\chi_{Q_{1}}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Alternatively, one may use a heteroskedasticity-robust Wald test of 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\eta=0$
\end_inset

 in the auxiliary model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{u}_{i}=\hat{r}_{i}^{\prime}\eta+\mbox{\emph{error}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This approach differs from the LM statistic in that residuals 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{e}_{i}\equiv\hat{u}_{i}-\hat{r}_{i}\hat{\eta}$
\end_inset

 are used in the implicit variance matrix estimator, rather than 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Under the null hypothesis (and local alternatives), 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\eta}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}0$
\end_inset

, and so the statistics are asymptotically equivalent.
\end_layout

